## Reading and printing

To read something typed by the user, you use `std::cin` with the `>>` operator. You can chain several outputs with `<<`:

```
int age;
std::cout << "Enter your age: ";
std::cin >> age;
std::cout << "You are " << age << " years old" << std::endl;
```

## using namespace std

To avoid repeating `std::` all the time, you can write `using namespace std;`. Then `cout` is enough.

```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    cout << "Hello" << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In small study programs, this is common. In larger projects, many people prefer writing `std::` explicitly, because importing the whole namespace can cause hard-to-track name collisions. It is worth knowing both forms.

# Declaring variables

C++ is statically typed: you declare the type before the name. The general form is `type name = value;`.

```
int age = 30;
double price = 19.90;
bool active = true;
char initial = 'A';
```

The type says what values the variable holds and how much space it takes. Unlike languages with garbage collectors, you should keep the memory cost of each type in mind for high-performance code.

## Text with std::string

For text, modern C++ uses the `std::string` type, from the `<string>` library. It is much safer and more practical than the character arrays inherited from the C language.

```
#include <string>

std::string name = "Ana";
std::cout << name << std::endl;
```

# The if

The `if` runs a block when the condition, in parentheses, is true. You chain cases with `else if` and cover the rest with `else`.

```
int grade = 75;
if (grade >= 90) {
    std::cout << "A";
} else if (grade >= 70) {
    std::cout << "B";   // executes
} else {
    std::cout << "C";
}
```

C++ tests conditions from top to bottom and runs the first block whose condition is true.

## The switch and break

The `switch` compares an integer (or character) value against several cases. Important: in C++, each `case` needs a `break`, otherwise execution **falls** into the next case (fall-through). Forgetting `break` is a classic source of bugs.

```
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        std::cout << "Sunday";
        break;
    case 3:
        std::cout << "Tuesday";
        break;
    default:
        std::cout << "Another day";
}
```

# while

The `while` repeats a block as long as the condition is true. The condition is tested **before** each repetition, so the block may not run at all.

```
int counter = 1;
while (counter <= 3) {
    std::cout << counter << " ";
    counter++;
}
// prints: 1 2 3
```

## do-while

The `do-while` tests the condition **after** executing the block, so the block runs at least once, even if the condition starts false.

```
int n = 10;
do {
    std::cout << n;   // prints 10 once
    n++;
} while (n <= 3);
```

