## The range-based for

Since C++11, when the goal is just to walk through the elements of a container (like a vector) or a string, the **range-based for** is cleaner. It reads as "for each element in".

```
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30};
for (int n : numbers) {
    std::cout << n << " ";   // 10 20 30
}
```

To iterate without copying each element (important for large objects), use a reference: `for (const auto& n : numbers)`.

# Declaring functions

A function in C++ declares the return type, the name, and the parameters with their types. If it returns nothing, the return type is `void`.

```
int somar(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

int main() {
    int total = somar(3, 4);   // 7
}
```

In C++, a function must be declared before it is used. So either you define it above `main`, or you declare a **prototype** (the signature without a body) at the top of the file.

## Pass-by-value: a copy

By default, C++ passes arguments **by value**: the function receives a copy of the argument. Changing the parameter inside the function does not affect the caller's original variable.

```
void tentarDobrar(int x) {
    x = x * 2;   // changes only the local copy
}

int n = 5;
tentarDobrar(n);
// n is still 5
```

Passing by value is simple and safe, but copying large objects can cost a lot in performance.

# References: an alias for the variable

A **reference**, marked with `&` in the parameter's type, makes the function work on the caller's **actual** variable, not on a copy. That way, the function can change it.

```
void dobrar(int& x) {
    x = x * 2;   // changes the original
}

int n = 5;
dobrar(n);
// n is now 10
```

The reference is one of C++'s core features and shows up all the time.

## Default arguments

You can give a parameter a **default** value. If the caller does not supply that argument, the default is used. Parameters with default values must come last.

```
void cumprimentar(std::string nome, std::string saudacao = "Olá") {
    std::cout << saudacao << ", " << nome;
}

cumprimentar("Ana");            // Olá, Ana
cumprimentar("Ana", "Oi");      // Oi, Ana
```

## Dangling pointers

The opposite problem: using a pointer **after** the memory has been freed. After a `delete`, the pointer still holds the old address, but the memory is no longer yours. Dereferencing that **dangling pointer** leads to undefined behavior, one of C++'s most treacherous bugs.

```
int* p = new int(10);
delete p;
// *p is now undefined behavior: p is dangling
```

# Modeling with classes

A **class** is a template that groups data (attributes) and behavior (methods) under one type. An **object** is a concrete instance of the class. C++ inherited object orientation from adding classes to the C language, and that is where this heritage shows up.

```
class Cachorro {
public:
    std::string nome;
    void latir() {
        std::cout << nome << " faz au au" << std::endl;
    }
};

Cachorro rex;
rex.nome = "Rex";
rex.latir();   // Rex faz au au
```

# The constructor

The **constructor** is a special method that runs when the object is created, to initialize it. It has the same name as the class and declares no return type.

```
class Cachorro {
public:
    std::string nome;
    int idade;

    Cachorro(std::string n, int i) {
        nome = n;
        idade = i;
    }
};

Cachorro rex("Rex", 3);   // calls the constructor
```

# The destructor: unique to C++

The **destructor** is a special method that runs automatically when the object is destroyed, whether by going out of scope (on the stack) or being released with `delete` (on the heap). It has the class name with a tilde in front: `~Cachorro()`. The destructor is the place to release resources the object acquired, such as memory, files, or connections.

```
class Arquivo {
public:
    Arquivo() { /* opens the file */ }
    ~Arquivo() { /* closes the file automatically */ }
};
```

This constructor/destructor pair is the foundation of one of C++'s most important concepts, RAII, in the next lesson.

# Encapsulation

**Encapsulation** means hiding the object's internal details and controlling access to them. In practice, you make attributes `private` and offer public methods to read and change them under rules. This protects the object from invalid states.

```
class ContaBancaria {
private:
    double saldo = 0;
public:
    double getSaldo() const { return saldo; }
    void depositar(double valor) {
        if (valor > 0) saldo += valor;   // only accepts positives
    }
};
```

Note the `const` after `getSaldo()`: it promises the method does not change the object.

# Reuse and specialize

**Inheritance** lets a class (the derived one) reuse the members of another (the base) and add or specialize whatever it needs. In C++, you use a colon and the inheritance type, usually `public`.

```
class Animal {
public:
    std::string nome;
    void comer() {
        std::cout << nome << " está comendo" << std::endl;
    }
};

class Gato : public Animal {
public:
    void miar() {
        std::cout << nome << " faz miau" << std::endl;
    }
};
```

# The problem virtual solves

In C++, for a base-class pointer or reference to call the derived class's version, the method must be declared **virtual** in the base. Without `virtual`, the call uses the base version, decided at compile time.

```
class Animal {
public:
    virtual void emitirSom() {
        std::cout << "Som genérico" << std::endl;
    }
};

class Gato : public Animal {
public:
    void emitirSom() override {
        std::cout << "Miau" << std::endl;
    }
};
```

The `override` keyword in the derived class makes it clear you are overriding a virtual method, and the compiler checks it.

# Pure virtual methods and abstract classes

A virtual method can be declared **pure**, without a body, by assigning `= 0`. A class with at least one pure virtual method is **abstract**: it cannot be instantiated, and it serves as a contract for derived classes, which are required to implement the method.

```
class Forma {
public:
    virtual double area() = 0;   // pure virtual: no body
};

class Circulo : public Forma {
public:
    double raio;
    double area() override { return 3.14159 * raio * raio; }
};
```

`Forma` cannot be created directly; `Circulo` implements `area()` and can.

## The virtual destructor: a classic trap

An important C++ rule: if you have a base class with virtual methods and will delete derived objects through a base pointer, the **base destructor must be virtual**. Without that, deleting through the base pointer calls only the base destructor, and the derived part is not released correctly, causing leaks.

```
class Base {
public:
    virtual ~Base() {}   // virtual destructor
};
```

# std::map: key and value

`std::map` associates keys with values, like a dictionary. You access and insert with square brackets.

```
#include <map>

std::map<std::string, int> idades;
idades["Ana"] = 30;
idades["Bruno"] = 25;
std::cout << idades["Ana"];   // 30
```

The type `std::map<std::string, int>` means: string keys, int values. `std::set` is a cousin that stores only **unique elements**, with no associated values, useful for guaranteeing there are no duplicates.

## unique_ptr and shared_ptr

**std::unique_ptr** represents exclusive ownership: only one smart pointer owns the resource, and it releases it automatically when destroyed. Create one with `std::make_unique`.

```
#include <memory>

auto p = std::make_unique<int>(42);
std::cout << *p << std::endl;   // 42
// no delete: the memory is freed on its own at the end of the scope
```

**std::shared_ptr** allows shared ownership: several of them can point to the same resource, which is released when the last one goes away (reference counting). Use it when ownership is truly shared.
